---
// Lesson 10.1d — 3-Button Jam: Friend Playtest & Triage
// Multi-round blind Friend Playtest protocol: watch friends discover the 3 buttons cold,
// capture empirical observations instead of excuses, and prioritize revisions cleanly.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.1d — 3-Button Jam: Friend Playtest &amp; Triage</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You have stared at your own scene for three days, so your brain already fills in every missing
    hint and unfair hitbox automatically. Today you rotate stations so <strong>three friends
    playtest your 3-Button game cold</strong> — and you practice the hardest skill in software:
    watching silently without explaining anything while someone breaks what you made.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Protocol: How to Run a Friend Playtest (~10 min prep)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li><strong>Before standing up:</strong> make sure your scene displays your 3 button keys right on screen (or on a tiny folded index card beside the keyboard) so you never have to explain them verbally.</li>
        <li><strong>The Silence Rule:</strong> press Play, step back half a chair, and DO NOT touch the mouse or speak while your tester plays for 90 seconds. If they ask "Wait, does J do anything right now?" answer kindly with "Keep trying what looks right" and write down that question!</li>
        <li><strong>Watch eyes + fingers, not just the screen:</strong> watch which keys their fingers hunt for, when they lean forward, when they look bored, and the exact moment they lose without understanding why.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Three Rotations: Gather Evidence (~35 min)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Swap seats with three different friends (about 10 minutes per pair: 3 min play + 2 min debrief each way):</p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Friend #</th><th>First 10 Seconds (Did They Get the 3 Buttons?)</th><th>Biggest Friction / Confusion Moment</th><th>Exact Bug or Balance Surprise</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Friend 1</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Friend 2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Friend 3</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        <strong>Good debrief questions to ask AFTER they finish:</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>"Which of the three buttons felt unnecessary or confusing?"</li>
        <li>"Tell me what you thought killed you on your fastest loss."</li>
        <li>"Did the game get harder in a way that felt exciting or random?"</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Triage: Rank Revisions Before Coding (~15 min)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Do not try to implement every suggestion a friend throws out ("add a shop! add multiplayer!").
        Sort every observation into priority buckets so your next work session makes the biggest leap:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>P0 — Broken / Unplayable:</strong> crashes, soft-locks, impossible starts, restart failures, buttons unresponsive.</li>
        <li><strong>P1 — Clarity &amp; Core Friction:</strong> player could not tell what hurt them, forgot what Button 3 does, difficulty ramped unfairly fast, hitboxes felt deceptive.</li>
        <li><strong>P2 — Feel &amp; Polish:</strong> audio feedback, color flashes, animation smoothing, particles.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Reading Playtest Data Honestly</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Name ONE surprise that happened to two or all three friends — why did it catch them off guard when it seemed completely obvious to you?</li>
            <li>Write down one polite friend suggestion you are intentionally choosing to IGNORE (say why it misses your core 3-button focus or blows your jam scope).</li>
            <li>State your concrete Revision Contract for next class: list your top P0/P1 fix, the exact script or scene change it requires, and how you will prove the revision worked.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Observe three classmates play your build silently and log concrete facts for each run</li>
        <li>Gather debrief answers without arguing with or defending your design</li>
        <li>Prioritize findings cleanly into P0 (broken), P1 (clarity/balance), and P2 (polish)</li>
        <li>Lock in a clear Revision Contract before touching code next class</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/index.html">Unit 10: Jam Season</a> — friend playtest &amp; triage rules</li>
        <li><a href="8.5.html">8.5 — Boss Playtest with Friends</a> — the boss playtest ritual this expands</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="10.1e.html">10.1e — 3-Button Jam: Revisions &amp; Polish</a> — fix the top blockers, prove the fix with a re-test, then freeze.</p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="10.1d" title="10.1d 3-Button Jam: Friend Playtest &amp; Triage — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
